// @test: typecheck-pass
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, mount, glob, name-resolution, stdlib
//
// Pin (T0969) — `mount X.*;` binds X's names even when the stdlib itself
// already depends on X.
//
// The failure was selective in a way that hides it from casual testing.  A
// glob over `core.intrinsics.bitwise` works; a glob over
// `core.intrinsics.control` imports NOTHING, silently, and the author reads
//
//     error<E100>: unbound variable: nop
//
// at the use site — which reads as a misspelled name, not a failed mount.
//
// Measured with a debug trace, the module has TWO registry entries:
//
//     Module 'core.intrinsics.control':  0 exports   <- empty stub
//     Module 'intrinsics.control':      20 exports   <- the real one
//
// The glob resolves its path to the `core.`-prefixed spelling, finds the
// stub, and imports its zero exports.  `bitwise` has no such stub, so the
// tolerant registry lookup reaches the real entry and the same syntax works.
//
// Which modules get a stub: exactly the ones the stdlib itself depends on.
// In one measured run, 90 of them — among the intrinsics, `atomic`,
// `control`, `runtime` and `runtime.async_ops`, but not `bitwise`, `float`,
// `arithmetic`, `memory`, `type_info`, `conversion`, `platform`, `simd`,
// `tensor`, `gpu` or `lowlevel`.  `core.base.panic` pulls in
// `core.intrinsics.control`; `core.sync.atomic` pulls in
// `core.intrinsics.atomic`.  So the rule a user meets is: a glob mount
// silently imports nothing the MORE central the module is.
//
// The named mount of the same item works throughout —
// `mount core.intrinsics.control.nop;` binds `nop` — because a named mount
// resolves through the archive metadata and the glob resolved through the
// registry.  Two spellings of one intent, answered by two different
// authorities, disagreeing.  That is what this spec pins: they must agree.
//
// `nop` is chosen because it is not ambient — see the companion
// `a_mounted_only_name_is_not_ambient.vr`, without which this spec could
// pass on a name the prelude already provides and measure nothing.

mount core.intrinsics.control.*;

fn main() {
    nop();
    let hot: Bool = likely(true);
    let _ = hot;
    print("ok");
}
